gm-method/rendering (LOD-swap pop-in/out flicker in a per-instance stateless-LOD instanced veg system): @three.ez/instanced-mesh assigns each instance's LOD level STATELESSLY from camera distance every frame; if addLOD is called with hysteresis=0 (the default), a tree hovering at a cutover distance toggles level frame-to-frame as the distance jitters -- and at the farthest cutover (mesh swaps to empty geo and a separate impostor mesh takes over) that toggle is drawn<->undrawn, i.e. trees pop in and out. Fix: thread a non-zero hysteresis fraction (~0.12) into EVERY addLOD distance (all mesh LOD bands, the empty-geo far swap, the per-species impostor fallback, AND the shared impostor mesh's own near<->far plane swap at its nearCutoff) so the band an instance currently sits in is sticky to sub-metre jitter. Subtlety: the lib's BVH-LOD path copies level.distance RAW (no hysteresis) and only re-applies hysteresis when the per-instance BVH box SPANS bands (getLevel returns null -> recompute via getObjectLODIndexForDistance); a tall tree union box near a boundary reliably spans bands, so the hysteresis does engage for the flicker case. Keep an overlap band at the mesh->impostor seam (impostor nearCutoff slightly inside the mesh's far cutover) so both draw in the seam and there is never a 1-frame neither-draws gap. Lock with a source-shape regression test asserting no addLOD is left at the implicit-0 3-arg form on the flicker-prone cutovers.